## Solution

We compute $\int x^{-2} J_3(x)\,dx$ using the standard Bessel recurrence.

**Key identity.** For Bessel functions of the first kind, the following differentiation formula holds for all $\nu$:

$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^{-\nu} J_\nu(x)\right] = -x^{-\nu} J_{\nu+1}(x).$$

*Proof of the identity.* From the two standard recurrences
$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^\nu J_\nu(x)\right] = x^\nu J_{\nu-1}(x), \qquad J_\nu'(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\bigl[J_{\nu-1}(x)-J_{\nu+1}(x)\bigr],$$
we differentiate $x^{-\nu}J_\nu(x)$ directly:
$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^{-\nu}J_\nu(x)\right] = -\nu\, x^{-\nu-1}J_\nu(x) + x^{-\nu}J_\nu'(x).$$
Using $J_\nu'(x)=\frac{\nu}{x}J_\nu(x)-J_{\nu+1}(x)$ (equivalent to the second recurrence above), the first term cancels:
$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^{-\nu}J_\nu(x)\right] = -\nu\,x^{-\nu-1}J_\nu(x) + x^{-\nu}\!\left[\frac{\nu}{x}J_\nu(x)-J_{\nu+1}(x)\right] = -x^{-\nu}J_{\nu+1}(x). \quad\checkmark$$

**Applying it.** Set $\nu = 2$. Then $x^{-\nu} = x^{-2}$ and $J_{\nu+1} = J_3$, so the identity gives

$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^{-2}J_2(x)\right] = -x^{-2}J_3(x).$$

Integrating both sides with respect to $x$:

$$x^{-2}J_2(x) = -\int x^{-2}J_3(x)\,dx,$$

hence

$$\boxed{\int x^{-2}J_3(x)\,dx = -x^{-2}J_2(x) + C.}$$

**Verification.** Differentiating the result:
$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[-x^{-2}J_2(x) + C\right] = -\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[x^{-2}J_2(x)\right] = -\!\left(-x^{-2}J_3(x)\right) = x^{-2}J_3(x),$$
which is exactly the integrand. $\checkmark$

